{"authored" [{"question" "q-49604" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{13}{(3x+2)(2x-3)}\\,\\text{d}x=a\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-3|+b\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+2|+C,\n$$\n\nfind the value of $a+b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042574/state.json" "file_sha256" "884c56d5f217782d1097ed06237fe2ab45675e0787500cf701f35dd3b22d35a3" "basis" 1560 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042574/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-49604" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{13}{(3x+2)(2x-3)} & =\\frac{A}{3x+2}+\\frac{B}{2x-3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n13=A(2x-3)+B(3x+2).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=\\frac{3}{2},$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13 & =A(2(\\frac{3}{2})-3)+B(3(\\frac{3}{2})+2) \\\\ 13 & =\\frac{13}{2}B \\\\ B & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-\\frac{2}{3},$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13 & =A(2(-\\frac{2}{3})-3)+B(3(-\\frac{2}{3})+2) \\\\ 13 & =-\\frac{13}{3}A \\\\ A & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{13}{(3x+2)(2x-3)}=-\\frac{3}{3x+2}+\\frac{2}{2x-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{13}{(3x+2)(2x-3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-\\frac{3}{3x+2}+\\frac{2}{2x-3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{3}{3x+2}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{2}{2x-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-3|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+2|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=1,b=-1,$ and $a+b=1-1=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042574/state.json" "file_sha256" "884c56d5f217782d1097ed06237fe2ab45675e0787500cf701f35dd3b22d35a3" "basis" 1560 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042574/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-49604" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042574/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d1f4250bdf92c2c10beeab68958ced148fb512341396ca5d2b86210b849b8d1b" "basis" 1560 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042574/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-49604" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042574/state.json" "file_sha256" "884c56d5f217782d1097ed06237fe2ab45675e0787500cf701f35dd3b22d35a3" "basis" 1560 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042574/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-49604" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042574/state.json" "file_sha256" "884c56d5f217782d1097ed06237fe2ab45675e0787500cf701f35dd3b22d35a3" "basis" 1560 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042574/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-49604" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042574/state.json" "file_sha256" "884c56d5f217782d1097ed06237fe2ab45675e0787500cf701f35dd3b22d35a3" "basis" 1560 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042574/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-49604" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042574/state.json" "file_sha256" "884c56d5f217782d1097ed06237fe2ab45675e0787500cf701f35dd3b22d35a3" "basis" 1560 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042574/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-49604" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042574/state.json" "file_sha256" "884c56d5f217782d1097ed06237fe2ab45675e0787500cf701f35dd3b22d35a3" "basis" 1560 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042574/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-49604" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042574/state.json" "file_sha256" "884c56d5f217782d1097ed06237fe2ab45675e0787500cf701f35dd3b22d35a3" "basis" 1560 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042574/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-49604" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042574/state.json" "file_sha256" "884c56d5f217782d1097ed06237fe2ab45675e0787500cf701f35dd3b22d35a3" "basis" 1560 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042574/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-349041" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{15x-39}{5{x}^{2}-19x-4}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042574/state.json" "file_sha256" "884c56d5f217782d1097ed06237fe2ab45675e0787500cf701f35dd3b22d35a3" "basis" 1560 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042574/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-349041" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that a denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n5{x}^{2}-19x-4=(5x+1)(x-4).\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{15x-39}{5{x}^{2}-19x-4} & =\\frac{15x-39}{(5x+1)(x-4)}=\\frac{A}{5x+1}+\\frac{B}{x-4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n15x-39=A(x-4)+B(5x+1).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15(4)-39 & =A(4-4)+B(5(4)+1) \\\\ 21 & =21B \\\\ B & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-\\frac{1}{5},$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15(-\\frac{1}{5})-39 & =A(-\\frac{1}{5}-4)+B(5(-\\frac{1}{5})+1) \\\\ -42 & =-\\frac{21}{5}A \\\\ A & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{15x-39}{5{x}^{2}-19x-4}=\\frac{10}{5x+1}+\\frac{1}{x-4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{15x-39}{5{x}^{2}-19x-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{10}{5x+1}+\\frac{1}{x-4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5x+1|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{15x-39}{5{x}^{2}-19x-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5x+1|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(5x+1)^{2}(x-4)|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{15x-39}{5{x}^{2}-19x-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(5x+1)^{2}(x-4)|{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(242)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{242}{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{121}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042574/state.json" "file_sha256" "884c56d5f217782d1097ed06237fe2ab45675e0787500cf701f35dd3b22d35a3" "basis" 1560 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042574/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-349041" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042574/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d1f4250bdf92c2c10beeab68958ced148fb512341396ca5d2b86210b849b8d1b" "basis" 1560 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042574/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-349041" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042574/state.json" "file_sha256" "884c56d5f217782d1097ed06237fe2ab45675e0787500cf701f35dd3b22d35a3" "basis" 1560 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042574/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-506" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int  \\dfrac{5x}{x^2+3x-4} \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042574/example-506.json" "file_sha256" "d2b767b6be16ca1d7fdc22f94e9b39480d3b2ef2cb85eb6aa41754fcfceb578f" "basis" 1560 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042574/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-506" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we note that the denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\nx^2+3x-4 = (x-1)(x+4) \\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x}{{x}^{2}+3x-4} & =\\frac{5x}{(x-1)(x+4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{A}{(x-1)}+\\frac{B}{(x+4)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x & =A(x+4)+B(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(-4) & =A(-4+4)+B(-4-1) \\\\ -20 & =-5B \\\\ B & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, by plugging in $x=1,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(1) & =A(1+4)+B(1-1) \\\\ 5 & =5A \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5x}{x^2+3x-4}  = \\dfrac{1}{(x-1)} +\\dfrac{4}{(x+4)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5x}{{x}^{2}+3x-4}\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{x-1}+\\frac{4}{x+4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\text{d}x+4\\int \\frac{1}{x+4}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042574/example-506.json" "file_sha256" "d2b767b6be16ca1d7fdc22f94e9b39480d3b2ef2cb85eb6aa41754fcfceb578f" "basis" 1560 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042574/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-507" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_1^4 \\dfrac{2x+3}{4x^2-1} \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042574/example-507.json" "file_sha256" "787d65b79d0b9c2555a626ba9876320c31f5baaa15c641a7221f48ecebb84e28" "basis" 1560 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042574/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-507" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that a denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n4x^2-1 = (2x+1)(2x-1) \\,.\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+3}{4{x}^{2}-1} & =\\frac{2x+3}{(2x+1)(2x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{A}{(2x+1)}+\\frac{B}{(2x-1)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+3 & =A(2x-1)+B(2x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=\\dfrac{1}{2},$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(\\frac{1}{2})+3 & =A(2(\\frac{1}{2})-1)+B(2(\\frac{1}{2})+1) \\\\ 4 & =2B \\\\ B & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-\\dfrac{1}{2},$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-\\frac{1}{2})+3 & =A(2(-\\frac{1}{2})-1)+B(2(-\\frac{1}{2})+1) \\\\ 2 & =-2A \\\\ A & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x+3}{4x^2-1} = -\\dfrac{1}{(2x+1)} +\\dfrac{2}{(2x-1)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+3}{4{x}^{2}-1}\\text{d}x & =\\int (-\\frac{1}{(2x+1)}+\\frac{2}{(2x-1)})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{1}{2x+1}\\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{2x-1}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+2(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|)+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+3}{4{x}^{2}-1}\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x+1}}|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{2x-1}{\\sqrt{2x+1}}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{2x+3}{4{x}^{2}-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡{|\\frac{2x-1}{\\sqrt{2x+1}}|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{3})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{3})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042574/example-507.json" "file_sha256" "787d65b79d0b9c2555a626ba9876320c31f5baaa15c641a7221f48ecebb84e28" "basis" 1560 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042574/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-49908" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$\\int \\frac{2}{3{x}^{2}+x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fce3676fc74d96c43e5be393fd38770b67fcb0a9c7ebf57b390e7a541bf7266"} {"question" "q-49908" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we note that the denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}+x=x(3x+1).\n$$\n\nWe convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x(3x+1)} & =\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{B}{(3x+1)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n2=A(3x+1)+Bx.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-\\frac{1}{3},$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =A(3(-\\frac{1}{3})+1)+B(-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\ 2 & =-\\frac{1}{3}B \\\\ 6 & =-B \\\\ B & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=0,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =A(3(0)+1)+B(0) \\\\ 2 & =A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x(3x+1)}=\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{6}{3x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{x(3x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{6}{3x+1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int \\frac{3}{3x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fce3676fc74d96c43e5be393fd38770b67fcb0a9c7ebf57b390e7a541bf7266"} {"question" "q-49908" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a0167a134041e89f4efa152a511cae7a330613f85b1f1130a98c461bc1bbe5a"} {"question" "q-49908" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fce3676fc74d96c43e5be393fd38770b67fcb0a9c7ebf57b390e7a541bf7266"} {"question" "q-49908" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|+C"] ["math" "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|+C"] ["math" "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|+C"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|+C"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|+C"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fce3676fc74d96c43e5be393fd38770b67fcb0a9c7ebf57b390e7a541bf7266"} {"question" "q-49908" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+1|+C"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fce3676fc74d96c43e5be393fd38770b67fcb0a9c7ebf57b390e7a541bf7266"} {"question" "q-49604" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{13}{(3x+2)(2x-3)}\\,\\text{d}x=a\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-3|+b\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+2|+C,\n$$\n\nfind the value of $a+b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fce3676fc74d96c43e5be393fd38770b67fcb0a9c7ebf57b390e7a541bf7266"} {"question" "q-49604" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{13}{(3x+2)(2x-3)} & =\\frac{A}{3x+2}+\\frac{B}{2x-3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n13=A(2x-3)+B(3x+2).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=\\frac{3}{2},$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13 & =A(2(\\frac{3}{2})-3)+B(3(\\frac{3}{2})+2) \\\\ 13 & =\\frac{13}{2}B \\\\ B & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-\\frac{2}{3},$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13 & =A(2(-\\frac{2}{3})-3)+B(3(-\\frac{2}{3})+2) \\\\ 13 & =-\\frac{13}{3}A \\\\ A & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{13}{(3x+2)(2x-3)}=-\\frac{3}{3x+2}+\\frac{2}{2x-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{13}{(3x+2)(2x-3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-\\frac{3}{3x+2}+\\frac{2}{2x-3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{3}{3x+2}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{2}{2x-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-3|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+2|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=1,b=-1,$ and $a+b=1-1=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fce3676fc74d96c43e5be393fd38770b67fcb0a9c7ebf57b390e7a541bf7266"} {"question" "q-49604" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a0167a134041e89f4efa152a511cae7a330613f85b1f1130a98c461bc1bbe5a"} {"question" "q-49604" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fce3676fc74d96c43e5be393fd38770b67fcb0a9c7ebf57b390e7a541bf7266"} {"question" "q-49604" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fce3676fc74d96c43e5be393fd38770b67fcb0a9c7ebf57b390e7a541bf7266"} {"question" "q-49604" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fce3676fc74d96c43e5be393fd38770b67fcb0a9c7ebf57b390e7a541bf7266"} {"question" "q-348933" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following indefinite integral and fill in the missing coefficients.\n\n$\\int \\frac{11}{(2x+5)(x-2)}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+5|\\,+\\,$\n{{field-2}}$\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|+C$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fce3676fc74d96c43e5be393fd38770b67fcb0a9c7ebf57b390e7a541bf7266"} {"question" "q-348933" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we note that the denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n(2x+5)(x-2).\n$$\n\nWe convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{11}{(2x+5)(x-2)} & =\\frac{A}{2x+5}+\\frac{B}{x-2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n11=A(x-2)+B(2x+5).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=2,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11 & =9B \\\\ B & =\\frac{11}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-\\frac{5}{2},$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11 & =-\\frac{9}{2}A \\\\ A & =-\\frac{22}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{11}{(2x+5)(x-2)}=-\\frac{22}{9(2x+5)}+\\frac{11}{9(x-2)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{11}{(2x+5)(x-2)}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{22}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{2x+5}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{11}{9}\\int \\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{9}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+5|+\\frac{11}{9}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-2|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fce3676fc74d96c43e5be393fd38770b67fcb0a9c7ebf57b390e7a541bf7266"} {"question" "q-348933" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a0167a134041e89f4efa152a511cae7a330613f85b1f1130a98c461bc1bbe5a"} {"question" "q-348933" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fce3676fc74d96c43e5be393fd38770b67fcb0a9c7ebf57b390e7a541bf7266"} {"question" "q-348933" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{11}{9}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fce3676fc74d96c43e5be393fd38770b67fcb0a9c7ebf57b390e7a541bf7266"} {"question" "q-348933" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fce3676fc74d96c43e5be393fd38770b67fcb0a9c7ebf57b390e7a541bf7266"} {"question" "q-348933" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{11}{9}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fce3676fc74d96c43e5be393fd38770b67fcb0a9c7ebf57b390e7a541bf7266"} {"question" "q-348898" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following indefinite integral and fill in the missing coefficients.\n\n$\\int \\frac{7x-11}{(x-8)(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-8|\\,+\\,$\n{{field-2}}$\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fce3676fc74d96c43e5be393fd38770b67fcb0a9c7ebf57b390e7a541bf7266"} {"question" "q-348898" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we note that the denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n(x-8)(x+1).\n$$\n\nWe convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7x-11}{(x-8)(x+1)} & =\\frac{A}{x-8}+\\frac{B}{x+1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n7x-11=A(x+1)+B(x-8).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=8,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(8)-11 & =A(8+1)+B(8-8) \\\\ 45 & =9A \\\\ A & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-1,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(-1)-11 & =A(-1+1)+B(-1-8) \\\\ -18 & =-9B \\\\ B & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{7x-11}{(x-8)(x+1)}=\\frac{5}{x-8}+\\frac{2}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{7x-11}{(x-8)(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\int \\frac{1}{x-8}\\,\\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-8|+2\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fce3676fc74d96c43e5be393fd38770b67fcb0a9c7ebf57b390e7a541bf7266"} {"question" "q-348898" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a0167a134041e89f4efa152a511cae7a330613f85b1f1130a98c461bc1bbe5a"} {"question" "q-348898" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fce3676fc74d96c43e5be393fd38770b67fcb0a9c7ebf57b390e7a541bf7266"} {"question" "q-348898" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fce3676fc74d96c43e5be393fd38770b67fcb0a9c7ebf57b390e7a541bf7266"} {"question" "q-348898" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fce3676fc74d96c43e5be393fd38770b67fcb0a9c7ebf57b390e7a541bf7266"} {"question" "q-348898" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fce3676fc74d96c43e5be393fd38770b67fcb0a9c7ebf57b390e7a541bf7266"} {"question" "q-49905" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{5x-1}{(x-3)(2x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x=a\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+b\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+C,\n$$\n\nfind the value of $a+b.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fce3676fc74d96c43e5be393fd38770b67fcb0a9c7ebf57b390e7a541bf7266"} {"question" "q-49905" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x-1}{(x-3)(2x+1)} & =\\frac{A}{x-3}+\\frac{B}{2x+1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n5x-1=A(2x+1)+B(x-3).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(-\\frac{1}{2})-1 & =A(2(-\\frac{1}{2})+1)+B(-\\frac{1}{2}-3) \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2} & =-\\frac{7}{2}B \\\\ B & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=3,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(3)-1 & =A(2(3)+1)+B(3-3) \\\\ 14 & =7A \\\\ A & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x-1}{(x-3)(2x+1)}=\\frac{2}{x-3}+\\frac{1}{2x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5x-1}{(x-3)(2x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2}{x-3}+\\frac{1}{2x+1})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{2}{2x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=2,b=\\frac{1}{2},$ and $a+b=2+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fce3676fc74d96c43e5be393fd38770b67fcb0a9c7ebf57b390e7a541bf7266"} {"question" "q-49905" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a0167a134041e89f4efa152a511cae7a330613f85b1f1130a98c461bc1bbe5a"} {"question" "q-49905" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fce3676fc74d96c43e5be393fd38770b67fcb0a9c7ebf57b390e7a541bf7266"} {"question" "q-49905" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{7}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fce3676fc74d96c43e5be393fd38770b67fcb0a9c7ebf57b390e7a541bf7266"} {"question" "q-49905" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fce3676fc74d96c43e5be393fd38770b67fcb0a9c7ebf57b390e7a541bf7266"} {"question" "q-348976" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-4}^{0}\\frac{5x-1}{{x}^{2}+4x-5}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fce3676fc74d96c43e5be393fd38770b67fcb0a9c7ebf57b390e7a541bf7266"} {"question" "q-348976" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that the denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+4x-5=(x+5)(x-1).\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x-1}{{x}^{2}+4x-5} & =\\frac{5x-1}{(x+5)(x-1)}=\\frac{A}{x+5}+\\frac{B}{x-1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n5x-1=A(x-1)+B(x+5).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=1,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(1)-1 & =A(1-1)+B(1+5) \\\\ 4 & =6B \\\\ B & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-5,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(-5)-1 & =A(-5-1)+B(-5+5) \\\\ -26 & =-6A \\\\ A & =\\frac{13}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{5x-1}{{x}^{2}+4x-5}=\\frac{13}{3(x+5)}+\\frac{2}{3(x-1)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5x-1}{{x}^{2}+4x-5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{13}{3(x+5)}+\\frac{2}{3(x-1)})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+5|+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5x-1}{{x}^{2}+4x-5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{13}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+5|+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{0}\\frac{5x-1}{{x}^{2}+4x-5}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{13}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+5|+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|]}_{-4}^{0} \\\\  & =(\\frac{13}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1))-(\\frac{13}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fce3676fc74d96c43e5be393fd38770b67fcb0a9c7ebf57b390e7a541bf7266"} {"question" "q-348976" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a0167a134041e89f4efa152a511cae7a330613f85b1f1130a98c461bc1bbe5a"} {"question" "q-348976" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fce3676fc74d96c43e5be393fd38770b67fcb0a9c7ebf57b390e7a541bf7266"} {"question" "q-348976" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{11}{3}\\ln(5)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fce3676fc74d96c43e5be393fd38770b67fcb0a9c7ebf57b390e7a541bf7266"} {"question" "q-349040" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\frac{36x-10}{12{x}^{2}-x-6}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fce3676fc74d96c43e5be393fd38770b67fcb0a9c7ebf57b390e7a541bf7266"} {"question" "q-349040" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that a denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n12{x}^{2}-x-6=(4x-3)(3x+2).\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{36x-10}{12{x}^{2}-x-6} & =\\frac{36x-10}{(4x-3)(3x+2)}=\\frac{A}{4x-3}+\\frac{B}{3x+2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n36x-10=A(3x+2)+B(4x-3).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=\\frac{3}{4},$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36(\\frac{3}{4})-10 & =A(3(\\frac{3}{4})+2)+B(4(\\frac{3}{4})-3) \\\\ 17 & =\\frac{17}{4}A \\\\ A & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-\\frac{2}{3},$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}36(-\\frac{2}{3})-10 & =A(3(-\\frac{2}{3})+2)+B(4(-\\frac{2}{3})-3) \\\\ -34 & =-\\frac{17}{3}B \\\\ B & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{36x-10}{12{x}^{2}-x-6}=\\frac{4}{4x-3}+\\frac{6}{3x+2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{36x-10}{12{x}^{2}-x-6}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{4}{4x-3}+\\frac{6}{3x+2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|4x-3|+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+2|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{36x-10}{12{x}^{2}-x-6}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|4x-3|+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|3x+2|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(4x-3)(3x+2)^{2}|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\frac{36x-10}{12{x}^{2}-x-6}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(4x-3)(3x+2)^{2}|{|}_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{49}{4})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(12) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{49}{48}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{49}{48}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fce3676fc74d96c43e5be393fd38770b67fcb0a9c7ebf57b390e7a541bf7266"} {"question" "q-349040" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a0167a134041e89f4efa152a511cae7a330613f85b1f1130a98c461bc1bbe5a"} {"question" "q-349040" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fce3676fc74d96c43e5be393fd38770b67fcb0a9c7ebf57b390e7a541bf7266"} {"question" "q-349040" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\ln(\\frac{49}{48})"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fce3676fc74d96c43e5be393fd38770b67fcb0a9c7ebf57b390e7a541bf7266"} {"question" "q-349041" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{15x-39}{5{x}^{2}-19x-4}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fce3676fc74d96c43e5be393fd38770b67fcb0a9c7ebf57b390e7a541bf7266"} {"question" "q-349041" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that a denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n5{x}^{2}-19x-4=(5x+1)(x-4).\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{15x-39}{5{x}^{2}-19x-4} & =\\frac{15x-39}{(5x+1)(x-4)}=\\frac{A}{5x+1}+\\frac{B}{x-4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n15x-39=A(x-4)+B(5x+1).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15(4)-39 & =A(4-4)+B(5(4)+1) \\\\ 21 & =21B \\\\ B & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-\\frac{1}{5},$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15(-\\frac{1}{5})-39 & =A(-\\frac{1}{5}-4)+B(5(-\\frac{1}{5})+1) \\\\ -42 & =-\\frac{21}{5}A \\\\ A & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{15x-39}{5{x}^{2}-19x-4}=\\frac{10}{5x+1}+\\frac{1}{x-4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{15x-39}{5{x}^{2}-19x-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{10}{5x+1}+\\frac{1}{x-4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5x+1|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{15x-39}{5{x}^{2}-19x-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5x+1|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-4|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(5x+1)^{2}(x-4)|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{15x-39}{5{x}^{2}-19x-4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(5x+1)^{2}(x-4)|{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(242)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{242}{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{121}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fce3676fc74d96c43e5be393fd38770b67fcb0a9c7ebf57b390e7a541bf7266"} {"question" "q-349041" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a0167a134041e89f4efa152a511cae7a330613f85b1f1130a98c461bc1bbe5a"} {"question" "q-349041" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fce3676fc74d96c43e5be393fd38770b67fcb0a9c7ebf57b390e7a541bf7266"} {"question" "q-349041" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\ln(\\frac{121}{2})"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fce3676fc74d96c43e5be393fd38770b67fcb0a9c7ebf57b390e7a541bf7266"} {"question" "q-49764" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "${\\int }_{-6}^{-5}\\frac{5x+29}{(x+7)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fce3676fc74d96c43e5be393fd38770b67fcb0a9c7ebf57b390e7a541bf7266"} {"question" "q-49764" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x+29}{(x+7)(x+4)}=\\frac{A}{(x+7)}+\\frac{B}{(x+4)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n5x+29=A(x+4)+B(x+7).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(-4)+29 & =A(-4+4)+B(-4+7) \\\\ 9 & =3B \\\\ B & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-7,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(-7)+29 & =A(-7+4)+B(-7+7) \\\\ -6 & =-3A \\\\ A & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{5x+29}{(x+7)(x+4)}=\\frac{2}{(x+7)}+\\frac{3}{(x+4)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5x+29}{(x+7)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{2}{x+7}+\\frac{3}{x+4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{x+7}\\,\\text{d}x+3\\int \\frac{1}{x+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+7|+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+7)^{2}|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+4)^{3}|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+7)^{2}(x+4)^{3}|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5x+29}{(x+7)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+7)^{2}|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+4)^{3}|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+7)^{2}(x+4)^{3}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-6}^{-5}\\frac{5x+29}{(x+7)(x+4)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(x+7)^{2}(x+4)^{3}|{|}_{-6}^{-5} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{2})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{3}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fce3676fc74d96c43e5be393fd38770b67fcb0a9c7ebf57b390e7a541bf7266"} {"question" "q-49764" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a0167a134041e89f4efa152a511cae7a330613f85b1f1130a98c461bc1bbe5a"} {"question" "q-49764" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fce3676fc74d96c43e5be393fd38770b67fcb0a9c7ebf57b390e7a541bf7266"} {"question" "q-49764" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3"] ["math" "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2"] ["math" "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡5"] ["math" "5\\operatorname{ln}⁡2"] ["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fce3676fc74d96c43e5be393fd38770b67fcb0a9c7ebf57b390e7a541bf7266"} {"question" "q-49764" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fce3676fc74d96c43e5be393fd38770b67fcb0a9c7ebf57b390e7a541bf7266"} {"question" "q-49769" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-2}^{-1}\\frac{x+1}{x(x+3)}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fce3676fc74d96c43e5be393fd38770b67fcb0a9c7ebf57b390e7a541bf7266"} {"question" "q-49769" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we convert the integrand into a sum of partial fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+1}{x(x+3)} & =\\frac{A}{x}+\\frac{B}{x+3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\nx+1=A(x+3)+Bx.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-3,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3+1 & =A(-3+3)+B(-3) \\\\ -2 & =-3B \\\\ B & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=0,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+1 & =A(0+3)+B(0) \\\\ 1 & =3A \\\\ A & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+1}{x(x+3)}=\\frac{1}{3x}+\\frac{2}{3(x+3)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x+1}{x(x+3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{3x}+\\frac{2}{3(x+3)})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{2}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x+1}{x(x+3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x(x+3)^{2}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}\\frac{x+1}{x(x+3)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x(x+3)^{2}|{|}_{-2}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fce3676fc74d96c43e5be393fd38770b67fcb0a9c7ebf57b390e7a541bf7266"} {"question" "q-49769" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8a0167a134041e89f4efa152a511cae7a330613f85b1f1130a98c461bc1bbe5a"} {"question" "q-49769" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fce3676fc74d96c43e5be393fd38770b67fcb0a9c7ebf57b390e7a541bf7266"} {"question" "q-49769" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3"] ["math" "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5"] ["math" "5\\operatorname{ln}⁡3"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fce3676fc74d96c43e5be393fd38770b67fcb0a9c7ebf57b390e7a541bf7266"} {"question" "q-49769" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fce3676fc74d96c43e5be393fd38770b67fcb0a9c7ebf57b390e7a541bf7266"} {"question" "e-506" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int  \\dfrac{5x}{x^2+3x-4} \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/example-506.json" "file_sha256" "34b2f54e2352d4462c057cfd8d77c91a2536b81d63ae33e96e8dd902842aa9bc"} {"question" "e-506" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we note that the denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\nx^2+3x-4 = (x-1)(x+4) \\,.\n$$\n\nSo, the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x}{{x}^{2}+3x-4} & =\\frac{5x}{(x-1)(x+4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{A}{(x-1)}+\\frac{B}{(x+4)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x & =A(x+4)+B(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-4,$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(-4) & =A(-4+4)+B(-4-1) \\\\ -20 & =-5B \\\\ B & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, by plugging in $x=1,$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(1) & =A(1+4)+B(1-1) \\\\ 5 & =5A \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5x}{x^2+3x-4}  = \\dfrac{1}{(x-1)} +\\dfrac{4}{(x+4)}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5x}{{x}^{2}+3x-4}\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{x-1}+\\frac{4}{x+4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x-1}\\text{d}x+4\\int \\frac{1}{x+4}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-1|+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+4|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/example-506.json" "file_sha256" "34b2f54e2352d4462c057cfd8d77c91a2536b81d63ae33e96e8dd902842aa9bc"} {"question" "e-507" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_1^4 \\dfrac{2x+3}{4x^2-1} \\,\\textrm{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/example-507.json" "file_sha256" "23794ebdb5035b58102f733cbcf9a7f6ab0bd4f837021b56d1c77d2de446cdf2"} {"question" "e-507" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We'll work out the indefinite integral first and then proceed with the definite integral.\n\nFirst, we note that a denominator of the integrand can be factored as\n\n$$\n4x^2-1 = (2x+1)(2x-1) \\,.\n$$\n\nSo the integrand can be converted into a sum of partial fractions,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+3}{4{x}^{2}-1} & =\\frac{2x+3}{(2x+1)(2x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{A}{(2x+1)}+\\frac{B}{(2x-1)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+3 & =A(2x-1)+B(2x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=\\dfrac{1}{2},$ we can eliminate $A$ and solve for $B\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(\\frac{1}{2})+3 & =A(2(\\frac{1}{2})-1)+B(2(\\frac{1}{2})+1) \\\\ 4 & =2B \\\\ B & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, plugging in $x=-\\dfrac{1}{2},$ we can eliminate $B$ and solve for $A\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-\\frac{1}{2})+3 & =A(2(-\\frac{1}{2})-1)+B(2(-\\frac{1}{2})+1) \\\\ 2 & =-2A \\\\ A & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x+3}{4x^2-1} = -\\dfrac{1}{(2x+1)} +\\dfrac{2}{(2x-1)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+3}{4{x}^{2}-1}\\text{d}x & =\\int (-\\frac{1}{(2x+1)}+\\frac{2}{(2x-1)})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{1}{2x+1}\\text{d}x+2\\int \\frac{1}{2x-1}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+2(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|)+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x+3}{4{x}^{2}-1}\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x+1}}|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x-1|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{2x-1}{\\sqrt{2x+1}}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{2x+3}{4{x}^{2}-1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡{|\\frac{2x-1}{\\sqrt{2x+1}}|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{3})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{3})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14052215/example-507.json" "file_sha256" "23794ebdb5035b58102f733cbcf9a7f6ab0bd4f837021b56d1c77d2de446cdf2"}] "usage" {"e-507" [] "q-349041" [] "q-49604" [] "e-506" []} "basis" 1764 